<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=chemotherapy">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chemotherapy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Cancer chemotherapy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chemotherapy without a modifier usually means the drug treatment of cancer, although Paul Ehrlich coined the term in the early 1900s for any use of chemicals to treat disease. Mustard gas, used as a chemical warfare agent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, proved a potent suppressor of blood production, and nitrogen mustards were studied at the Yale School of Medicine during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>. In December 1942 several people with advanced lymphomas were given the drug by vein, and their improvement, though temporary, was marked. The survivors of a German air raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>, exposed to mustard gas, were found to have very low white blood cell counts, and mustine became the first drug developed from this research.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chemotherapy.jpg" alt="Chemotherapy" width="330" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bill Branson (Photographer) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chemotherapy_vials_(4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>December 1942</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Yale School of Medicine</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>In the broader sense of the term, the first modern chemotherapeutic agent was arsphenamine, an arsenic compound discovered in 1907 and used to treat syphilis.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons-in-world-war-i">Chemical weapons<span>August 1914</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=chemotherapy">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
